          "secNumber": "2.1",
          "heading": "Unit overview and conceptual map",
          "content": "The second law becomes useful when the equilibrium criterion is matched to the apparatus. In a closed system with controlled T,P, the combined thermal and mechanical reservoirs justify a Gibbs-energy minimum. In a rigid vessel with controlled T,V, a Helmholtz description is generally appropriate. Applying the wrong potential can change the predicted order of candidate states, especially when density, chemical composition or reservoir exchange is significant. The natural-variable statement is a compact way of recording boundary conditions, not a trick of notation.\n\nAn equilibrium calculation can answer which phase should eventually be favored without locating the saddle-point barrier between structures. That distinction explains supercooling, precipitation delays and retained phases. If a free-energy plot shows two minima, local curvature determines small-fluctuation stability while the global minimum determines equilibrium preference within the candidate set. Inspecting curvature at one composition cannot replace a common-tangent construction when a mixture of distant compositions lowers the overall free energy.",
